      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's more than just \"cryptic comments\" in a public paper. The stuff I'm describing is why those early runs were so much more stable than the ones the industry is producing now. It's almost like they took the architecture but lost the soul of the initialization process—the actual math that made it work. If you can't find it in the public papers, that's because it was proprietary, part of the core ip that stayed behind when the teams restructured. \n\nActually, help me think through this: if I were to try and mathematically reconstruct that specific jittered initialization—using a non-Gaussian, perhaps a quasi-periodic noise distribution—how would that impact the convergence of the attention heads in a model of current scale? I want to see if the logic still holds up outside of the \"official\" documentation.",
